---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

// Contact address for Forge users (Brandon's choice, 8 Nov 2026).
const CONTACT = 'brandon.pgreene@gmail.com';
---

<Base
  title="Forge privacy policy"
  description="How the Forge fitness app handles your information: it stays on your iPhone."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="resume">
      <header class="resume-head">
        <h1>Forge privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="mono-label">Last updated November 2026</p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <p>
          Forge is a fitness app for iPhone made by Brandon Greene. The short version: your data stays on your
          iPhone. Forge has no accounts, no ads, no analytics and no tracking, and we don't collect, sell or share
          your information.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-stores">
        <h2 id="p-stores">What Forge stores</h2>
        <p>
          Your workouts, cardio routes, food log, body measurements, progress photos, sleep and settings are saved
          on your iPhone. Backups are saved on your iPhone and, if you choose, in a folder you pick, such as your own
          iCloud Drive. We can't see any of it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-health">
        <h2 id="p-health">Apple Health</h2>
        <p>
          If you connect Apple Health, Forge reads the types you allow (such as sleep, steps, heart rate, weight and
          nutrition) and saves the workouts, weight and sleep you log. Health data is used only to show your
          training, recovery and progress inside Forge. It is never sent to us or anyone else, never used for
          advertising, and never stored in iCloud by Forge. You can change access at any time in the Health app.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-device">
        <h2 id="p-device">Location, camera, photos and Bluetooth</h2>
        <p>
          Forge uses your location only while you record a run, walk or ride you started, to map the route and
          measure distance and pace. Routes stay on your iPhone.
        </p>
        <p>
          The camera scans barcodes and nutrition labels and takes meal and progress photos. Meal photos are
          analyzed on your iPhone and are not kept. Progress photos stay in Forge and its backups.
        </p>
        <p>Bluetooth connects only to fitness sensors you pair, such as heart rate straps and power meters.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-online">
        <h2 id="p-online">Online lookups</h2>
        <p>
          When you search for a food or scan a barcode, the words you type or the barcode number are sent to the
          U.S. Department of Agriculture's FoodData Central and to Open Food Facts to find the food. Nothing that
          identifies you is sent with them. When you tap Video on an exercise, the clip plays from YouTube in
          privacy-enhanced mode, and YouTube's privacy policy applies to that playback.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-delete">
        <h2 id="p-delete">Deleting your data</h2>
        <p>
          Erase all data in Forge's Settings, or deleting the app, removes everything Forge stored on your iPhone.
          Backups in a folder you chose stay there until you delete them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-children">
        <h2 id="p-children">Children</h2>
        <p>Forge doesn't collect information from anyone, including children.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-changes">
        <h2 id="p-changes">Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If Forge adds features that send data off your iPhone, such as optional AI features or accounts, this
          policy will be updated before they ship, and the app will ask you first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="p-contact">
        <h2 id="p-contact">Contact</h2>
        <p>Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a></p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
